( '6.1', 'CASE 6.1.124', 'Delivery Vehicles as Non-Current Assets', 'Analyze why current assets are expected to convert into cash within the normal operating cycle. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['If part of a packaging manufacturer''s ten-year bank loan becomes due within the next twelve months, that portion should move into current liabilities.', 'A development patent is excluded from non-current assets because it has no physical substance.', 'A clothing retailer that takes out a corporate debenture repayable in more than one year classifies the obligation as a non-current liability.', 'A development patent is classified as a current asset because, being intangible, it is easily converted into cash within a year.', 'A clothing retailer that owes overdraft borrowings due within the next year classifies the obligation as a current liability.'], ARRAY[true, false, true, false, true], ARRAY['TRUE — The claim is correct. If part of a packaging manufacturer''s ten-year bank loan becomes due within the next twelve months, that portion should move into current liabilities.

The underlying topic is the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. When figures appear, the exam checks whether you can interpret them in accounting terms—what increased, what ratio is implied, and whether the label (current, non-current, equity, and so on) fits.

Apply the case evidence: A ten-year bank loan nearing its due date within a year has that portion reclassified into current liabilities for a packaging manufacturer. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

No qualifying word breaks the definition or scenario, so the answer is true.', 'FALSE — The claim is false. A development patent is excluded from non-current assets because it has no physical substance.

The task tests the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. Financial statements distinguish stocks (balance sheet at a date) from flows (income and cash over a period); many false statements swap those roles.

Apply the case evidence: Physical substance is not required for non-current classification; a development patent qualifies through its long-term value. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'TRUE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is correct. A clothing retailer that takes out a corporate debenture repayable in more than one year classifies the obligation as a non-current liability.

The underlying topic is the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. When figures appear, the exam checks whether you can interpret them in accounting terms—what increased, what ratio is implied, and whether the label (current, non-current, equity, and so on) fits.

Apply the case evidence: Repayment due beyond one year places the corporate debenture among the non-current liabilities of a clothing retailer. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'FALSE — This statement is false. A development patent is classified as a current asset because, being intangible, it is easily converted into cash within a year.

This is an accounting reasoning item on the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. Decide whether the sentence correctly describes how a transaction, balance-sheet line, or period result affects assets, liabilities, or equity.

From the figures or classification rule involved, a development patent is a non-current intangible asset because it provides benefit over several years, not because it converts to cash quickly. Accounting statements fail when a loss is treated as increasing equity, when a flow is placed on the balance sheet, or when a ratio inequality is reversed.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'TRUE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is correct. A clothing retailer that owes overdraft borrowings due within the next year classifies the obligation as a current liability.

The underlying topic is the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. When figures appear, the exam checks whether you can interpret them in accounting terms—what increased, what ratio is implied, and whether the label (current, non-current, equity, and so on) fits.

Apply the case evidence: Settlement within one year places the overdraft borrowings among the current liabilities of a clothing retailer. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

No qualifying word breaks the definition or scenario, so the answer is true.'], '5/5', 124, 'full' ),
